Inside a hollow conducting sphere under electrostatic equilibrium, all excess charges reside on the outer surface of the conductor. Applying Gauss's law to any Gaussian surface entirely inside the hollow cavity, \[\oint \vec{E}\cdot d\vec{A}
=
\frac{Q_{\text{enclosed}}}{\varepsilon_0}
=
0.\] Hence, \[\vec{E}=0.\] Therefore, the correct option is \[\boxed{A}\] <div class="sol-block sol-keypoints"><div class="sol-block-title">Key points</div><ul><li>Electric field inside a conductor at electrostatic equilibrium is zero.</li><li>All excess charge resides on the outer surface of the conductor.</li><li>From Gauss's law, \[\oint \vec{E}\cdot d\vec{A}
=
\frac{Q_{\text{enclosed}}}{\varepsilon_0}.\]</li><li>Since no charge is enclosed inside the cavity, \[\vec{E}=0.\]</li></ul></div>

