In delta connection, the full line voltage appears across each phase: \[V_{p\Delta}=V_L\] Hence, the starting current in delta is \[(I_{st})_{\Delta}=\frac{V_L}{Z_{sc}}\] In star connection, the phase voltage is \[V_{pY}=\frac{V_L}{\sqrt{3}}\] Therefore, the starting current in star is \[(I_{st})_{Y}=\frac{V_L}{\sqrt{3}\,Z_{sc}}\] Since \[T_{st}\propto (I_{st})^2,\] we get \[\frac{(T_{st})_{Y}}{(T_{st})_{\Delta}}
=
\left(
\frac{(I_{st})_{Y}}
     {(I_{st})_{\Delta}}
\right)^2
=
\left(
\frac{1}{\sqrt{3}}
\right)^2
=
\frac{1}{3}\] Given that the starting torque in delta connection is \[(T_{st})_{\Delta}=120\,\text{N-m},\] the starting torque in star connection is \[(T_{st})_{Y}
=
\frac{1}{3}\times 120
=
40\,\text{N-m}\] \[\boxed{A}\]

