questions.explanation:
The power angle difference is \[\delta =10^\circ\] Since machine 1 leads machine 2 in angle, real power flows from machine 1 to machine 2. Therefore machine 1 delivers real power. Hence \[(a)\rightarrow \text{Positive real power}\] Since \[|V_1|=0.95
&lt;
|V_2|=1.0\] machine 1 absorbs reactive power from machine 2. Hence \[(b)\rightarrow \text{Negative reactive power}\] A machine absorbing reactive power operates at lagging power factor. Therefore \[(c)\rightarrow \text{Lagging power factor}\] Hence, the correct matching is \[\boxed{
a\rightarrow P,\;
b\rightarrow S,\;
c\rightarrow U
}\]

Communication interference in a transmission line is primarily caused by the electrostatic induction due to unequal voltage distribution among the conductors. For an untransposed three-phase line, unequal spacing results in unequal inductance and capacitance of the three phases. Consequently, the phase voltages become unbalanced, leading to communication interference. However, if the three conductors are placed at the corners of an equilateral triangle, each conductor has identical spacing from the other two conductors. Therefore, \[D_{ab}=D_{bc}=D_{ca}\] which gives identical inductance and capacitance for all three phases. As a result, <ul><li>the three-phase receiving-end voltages remain balanced, and</li><li>the electric fields around the conductors are symmetrical, eliminating communication interference.</li></ul> Hence, equilateral spacing provides both balanced receiving-end voltage and freedom from communication interference. \[\boxed{
\begin{aligned}
\text{(A)}\quad
&amp;\text{Balanced receiving-end voltage} \\
&amp;\text{and no communication interference}
\end{aligned}
}\] <div class="sol-block sol-keypoints"><div class="sol-block-title">Key points</div><ul><li>Equilateral spacing makes the mutual spacing between all conductors identical.</li><li>Equal spacing results in equal inductance and capacitance of all three phases.</li><li>Balanced line parameters ensure balanced receiving-end voltage and negligible communication interference.</li></ul></div> <div class="sol-block sol-mistakes"><div class="sol-block-title">Mistakes to avoid</div><ul><li>Do not confuse equilateral spacing with transposition. Both improve symmetry, but equilateral spacing alone already provides identical phase geometry.</li><li>Communication interference is mainly associated with unbalanced electrostatic fields caused by unequal conductor spacing.</li><li>Equal conductor spacing directly leads to balanced electrical parameters and balanced voltages.</li></ul></div>

The average output voltage of a three-phase fully controlled converter considering source inductance (overlap angle \(\mu\)) is \[(V_o)_{\text{avg}}
=
\frac{3V_m}{2\pi}
\left[
\cos\alpha+\cos(\alpha+\mu)
\right]\] As the overlap angle \(\mu\) increases, \[\alpha+\mu \uparrow\] which results in \[\cos(\alpha+\mu)\downarrow.\] Hence, \[(V_o)_{\text{avg}}
=
\frac{3V_m}{2\pi}
\left[
\cos\alpha+\cos(\alpha+\mu)
\right]\] decreases with increase in overlap angle. Therefore, the correct option is \[\boxed{\text{A}}\]

In a PMMC (Permanent Magnet Moving Coil) instrument, deflecting torque is: \[T_d=NBIA\] Strong permanent magnets produce high magnetic flux density(B): Hence large torque is produced even for small current. Also, the moving system (coil + pointer) is very light. Therefore: \[\frac{\text{Torque}}{\text{Weight of moving parts}}\] is high. This gives: \[\text{High sensitivity, quick response and better accuracy}\] Hence, an important advantage of PMMC instruments is: \[\boxed{
\text{High torque/weight ratio}
}\] <strong>Why other options are wrong:</strong> <strong>A. Free from friction error</strong> \(\times\) PMMC instruments use pivots and bearings, so friction error is reduced but not completely eliminated. <strong>C. Low torque/weight ratio</strong> \(\times\) This is opposite to the actual PMMC characteristic. <strong>D. Can be used on both AC and DC</strong> \(\times\) PMMC instruments work only on DC because for AC torque reverses every half cycle. Hence average torque becomes zero. \[\boxed{
\textbf{B. }\text{ has high (torque/weight of moving parts) ratio}
}\]

A uniform scale means: \[\text{Equal change in input}
\Rightarrow
\text{Equal change in deflection}\] i.e., \[\theta\propto X\] For a PMMC instrument, deflecting torque is: \[T_d=NBIA\] Hence, \[T_d\propto I\] Controlling torque: \[T_c=K\theta\] At equilibrium: \[T_d=T_c
\Rightarrow I\propto\theta
\Rightarrow \theta\propto I\] Thus PMMC instruments have: \[\boxed{
\text{Uniform (linear) scale}
}\] <strong>Why other options are wrong:</strong> <strong>A. Moving iron</strong> \(\times\) For moving iron instruments: \[T_d\propto I^2
\Rightarrow \theta\propto I^2\] Hence scale is nonlinear and crowded near zero. <strong>B. Induction</strong> \(\times\) Induction instruments are mainly used for energy meters. <strong>D. Dynamometer</strong> \(\times\) Scale is generally non-uniform because torque depends on product of currents. The correct answer is: \[\boxed{
\textbf{C. }\text{ moving coil permanent magnet}
}\]

In an induction type ammeter, deflecting torque is produced due to interaction between: i) induced eddy currents ii) alternating magnetic flux Deflecting torque: \[T_d\propto\phi_1\phi_2\sin\delta\] For a fixed frequency: \[\delta=\text{constant}\] Also, magnetic flux is proportional to current: \[\phi\propto I\] Therefore, \[\boxed{
T_d\propto I^2
}\] The correct answer is: \[\boxed{
\textbf{A. }current^2
}\]

